WEBVTT

00:00:06.379 --> 00:00:12.980
And run into our enemy, and
it will fly off into the distance.

00:00:12.981 --> 00:00:16.125
Now the problem here is
that our power-up basically

00:00:16.137 --> 00:00:18.860
lasts forever, and
that's not entirely fair.

00:00:18.861 --> 00:00:21.300
So we're actually going
to add a timer in so

00:00:21.312 --> 00:00:25.300
that we can remove this
power-up at a certain time.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.765
In our player controller
script, we're going

00:00:28.777 --> 00:00:35.000
to go down underneath our on-trigger-enter,
and we're going to create a new method.

00:00:35.001 --> 00:00:38.160
So this method is going
to be a little strange.

00:00:38.259 --> 00:00:43.600
It's called an iInumerator.

00:00:43.601 --> 00:00:46.069
You might notice that after
adding the iInumerator type,

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:49.800
your IDE highlights it with
a red squiggly error line.

00:00:49.801 --> 00:00:53.960
If you check Unity's console, you'll likely
see an error message saying the type or

00:00:53.961 --> 00:00:57.360
namespace name
iInumerator could not be found.

00:00:57.361 --> 00:01:00.680
Are you missing a using
directive or an assembly reference?

00:01:00.681 --> 00:01:04.120
There's no need to worry,
however, because this is a simple fix.

00:01:04.121 --> 00:01:07.080
The issue here is that we're
missing a using directive.

00:01:07.081 --> 00:01:09.785
In C#, using directives
are lines at the beginning

00:01:09.797 --> 00:01:14.360
of a script that specify which name
spaces your code needs access to.

00:01:14.361 --> 00:01:16.342
So adding the right
using statement lets your

00:01:16.354 --> 00:01:20.400
code know where to look for
the type that you're trying to use.

00:01:20.401 --> 00:01:24.960
In this case, we need to add the
using system.collection directive.

00:01:24.961 --> 00:01:27.508
So go ahead and add
that line at the top of your

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.920
script, along with the
other using statement.

00:01:29.921 --> 00:01:33.120
Once you've added it, the error
in your IDE should disappear.

00:01:33.121 --> 00:01:35.421
Once you save your script,
then return to Unity, you'll

00:01:35.422 --> 00:01:39.040
see that the console error
has been resolved as well.

00:01:39.041 --> 00:01:44.240
With that out of the way, let's jump
back into working with Cobra Teens.

00:01:48.559 --> 00:01:55.760
That will call our power
up countdown routine.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:03.800
Now an iInumerator is something in
programming and C# known as an interface.

00:02:03.974 --> 00:02:10.240
In this case, it's not entirely necessary
to go into how it works or what it does, but

00:02:10.241 --> 00:02:13.994
it is very helpful to
be able to do certain

00:02:14.006 --> 00:02:21.800
things like enable a countdown
timer outside of our update loop.

00:02:21.801 --> 00:02:26.840
The way we're able to use this countdown
timer outside of our update loop is through

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:34.520
use of something called Cobra Teens that
will be able to run with a function called

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:36.280
wait for seconds.

00:02:36.281 --> 00:02:39.819
Now to be able to
make this entire process

00:02:39.831 --> 00:02:43.720
work, we actually need
to use a keyword called

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:48.371
yield, and so this will
actually enable us to

00:02:48.383 --> 00:02:52.961
run this timer in a place
outside of our update loop.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:04.360
So if we type yield, return, new, and
we're creating a new wait for seconds.

00:03:04.361 --> 00:03:09.120
And we'll wait for, let's call
it a seven or so seconds.

00:03:09.121 --> 00:03:15.720
So now our iInumerator is going to create a
new thread outside of our update loop, and

00:03:15.721 --> 00:03:19.125
it will use the wait
for seconds method to

00:03:19.137 --> 00:03:22.880
wait for seven seconds
before we do something.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:29.920
Now, it'll wait for seven seconds, and then
eventually once that timer is stopped, we

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:39.040
can set our has power up to false, and then
we're able to reset our player's state back

00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:42.760
to before they picked up the power up.

00:03:42.761 --> 00:03:48.240
Now in order to actually use this method,
we then need to use another method, which

00:03:48.241 --> 00:03:50.493
will put inside of our
on trigger enter in our

00:03:50.505 --> 00:03:52.720
if statement, because
that's when the players

00:03:52.845 --> 00:03:56.010
picked up the power
up, we're going to start

00:03:56.022 --> 00:03:59.200
a co routine, which
will actually start that

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:03.560
thread, start that loop
outside of our update method.

00:04:03.561 --> 00:04:09.800
And then we call our
power up countdown routine.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.983
So it'll start another
thread, use the power

00:04:13.995 --> 00:04:18.040
up countdown routine,
wait for seven seconds,

00:04:18.041 --> 00:04:22.320
set our power up to false, and then
we'll be back to the way we started.

00:04:22.321 --> 00:04:25.337
So then we go in our
game, let's take a look

00:04:25.349 --> 00:04:28.240
at the player and just
watch the has power

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:34.637
up variable, watch
that checkbox, pick up

00:04:34.649 --> 00:04:41.560
the power up, enemy goes away, and
eventually has power up should turn off.

00:04:41.561 --> 00:04:44.080
There you go.

00:04:44.081 --> 00:04:50.260
So what you're going to do in your player
controller script, you're going to add this

00:04:50.261 --> 00:04:55.160
line of code to create
an I enumerator method.

00:04:55.161 --> 00:05:00.680
In this I enumerator method, make sure
to use yield return new wait for seconds.

00:05:00.681 --> 00:05:03.810
That way it waits for a
certain amount of time

00:05:03.822 --> 00:05:08.080
before it actually does this
sets our power up to false.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:14.120
And then in our on trigger enter, one we've
picked up a power up, we start a co routine

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:21.120
and use our power up countdown
routine to start that countdown timer.

00:05:21.121 --> 00:05:22.121
Now it's your turn.